{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/kwgmr8","slide":1,"text":"[Sources]\n[1] Bain & Company × Myntra, E-Styling India: Decoding Online Fashion and Lifestyle Shopping Trends (August 2024): 175M+ online lifestyle shoppers; $16–17B current e-lifestyle market; $40–45B projected by 2028; roughly 1 in 5 lifestyle dollars expected online by 2028. https://preprodcms.bain.com/insights/e-styling-india-decoding-online-fashion-and-lifestyle-shopping-trends-webinar/\n[2] Moneycontrol / ANI (December 4, 2025): ~200M annual active users expected by end-2025; 4M+ trend-first styles; +25% YoY repeat-customer growth; increased purchase frequency; 50% active customer base is Gen Z. https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/companies/myntra-set-to-hit-200-million-annual-active-users-in-2025-50-of-the-active-customer-base-is-gen-z-13713290.html\n[3] The Economic Times / Registrar of Companies (September 12, 2025): ₹6,043 crore FY25 revenue from operations; +18% YoY. https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/tech/technology/myntras-net-profit-rises-to-rs-548-crore-in-fy25-revenue-up-18/articleshow/123855941.cms\n[4] Mint, reporting Datum Intelligence estimates: selected 2025 online-fashion shares — Flipkart 25.2%, Myntra 17.1%, Amazon 13.8%, AJIO 9.0%, Nykaa Fashion 2.3%. Meesho and other players intentionally omitted. https://www.livemint.com/companies/nykaa-fashion-gmv-growth-profitability-11781421999258.html\n[5] The Ken, The rise, fall, and return of GMV (February 10, 2024): industry analysis that maturing Indian e-commerce increases the importance of cross-selling, repeat purchases, and protecting customer wallet share; fashion e-commerce faced difficult growth conditions. https://the-ken.com/the-nutgraf/the-rise-fall-and-return-of-gmv/\n[6] Graduation Project Brief supplied by the project owner: Growth Team goal to increase the percentage of users who purchase at least one wishlist item within 30 days of adding it.\n[/Sources]"}